nd (3,1). Let's see if there is a sunny line through (2,2) and (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1/1 = -1, prohibited. So not allowed. Could L2 cover (2,2) and (1,1) (already covered) slope = (1-2)/(1-2) = -1/ -1 = 1, allowed. So L2 can be line through (2,2) and (1,1). That covers (2,2) and (1,1). So all points are covered: (1,1) covered by L0 and L2; (1,2) covered by L0; (1,3) covered by L0 and L1; (2,1) covered by L1; (2,2) covered by L2; (3,1) not covered yet! Wait (3,1) is not covered by any line: L0 is x=1, L1 is line through (2,1) and (1,3), L2 is line through (2,2) and (1,1). Does L1 or L2 cover (3,1)? L1 passes through (2,1) and (1,3). Equation: slope = -2, passes through (2,1): y-1 = -2(x-2) => y = -2x +5. For x=3, y = -6+5 = -1, not 1. So (3,1) not on L1. L2 passes through (2,2) and (1,1): slope = 1, equation y-1 = 1*(x-1) => y = x. For x=3, y=3, not 1. So (3,1) not covered. So this configuration fails.\n\nTry different non-sunny line: L0 = horizontal y=1, covering (2,1),(3,1). Then we need to cover (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,2) with two sunny lines. Could L1 be line through (1,1) and (2,2) slope = 1, allowed. L2 could be line through (1,2) and (1,3) vertical, not allowed. Could be line through (1,2) and (2,2) slope = (2-2)/(2-1) = 0, horizontal, not allowed. Could be line through (1,2) and (2,1) slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, prohibited. Could be line through (1,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So L2 can be line through (1,2) and (3,1). But (3,1) already covered by L0, okay. So L2 covers (1,2) and (3,1). L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Points left: (1,3) not covered. Could be covered by L2? L2 passes through (1,2) and (3,1), not (1,3). Could be covered by L1? L1 passes through (1,1) and (2,2), not (1,3). So (1,3) remains uncovered. Could we adjust L2 to cover (1,3) and some other point? Let's try L2: line through (1,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. That covers (1,3) and (2,1) (already covered by L0). Then we need to cover (1,2) and (2,2) maybe